            <div xml:id="d530">

                <pb facs="0713" n="599" sameAs="frus:frus1922v02/pg599"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862i.01/185a</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Japanese Ambassador</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Shidehara</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1922-02-11"><hi rendition="#italic">February 11,
                        1922</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Excellency</hi>: In proceeding this day to the
                    signature of the Convention between the United States and Japan with respect to
                    former German Possessions under a Mandate to Japan, I have the honor to state
                    that if in the future the Government of the United States should have occasion
                    to make any commercial treaties applicable to Australia and New Zealand, it will
                    seek to obtain an extension of <pb facs="0714" n="600" xml:id="pg600"/> such
                    treaties to the mandated islands south of the Equator, now under the
                    Administration of those Dominions. I should add that the Government of the
                    United States has not yet entered into a convention for the giving of its
                    consent to the Mandate with respect to these islands.</p>
                <p>I have the honor further to state that it is the intention of the Government of
                    the United States, in making conventions, relating to former German territories
                    under mandate, to request that the governments holding mandates should address
                    to the United States, as one of the Principal Allied and Associated Powers,
                    duplicates of the annual reports of the administration of their mandates.</p>
                <p>Accept [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles E. Hughes</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
